1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,171
In the last video, many asked for OpenClaw.

2
00:00:02,171 --> 00:00:04,759
Well, since I'm such a fan-pleaser, I'll add one more.

3
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:07,620
there are already many guides online for setting up OpenClaw

4
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:10,830
Today I’ll show you something new—let’s see what it does

5
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:11,824
behind the scenes

6
00:00:11,824 --> 00:00:13,260
once it gets your message.

7
00:00:13,470 --> 00:00:14,285
But before that,

8
00:00:14,285 --> 00:00:17,440
let me briefly share my setup method—just skim through it,

9
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,670
since I think it'll fade out soon anyway.

10
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:21,836
OpenClaw is much like regular Agents

11
00:00:21,836 --> 00:00:23,922
in essence; it can be deployed locally,

12
00:00:23,922 --> 00:00:25,840
say on a Mac, or on a cloud server

13
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,564
the key difference is it can link to social media,

14
00:00:28,564 --> 00:00:30,760
and tasks are triggered via posted messages.

15
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:34,900
My setup: Tencent Cloud Lite VM + OpenRouter, LLM + Feishu.

16
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:36,560
Why not use all one brand's tools?

17
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:38,660
Then no one would think I'm just pushing ads

18
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:40,126
and these three are indeed the tools

19
00:00:40,126 --> 00:00:41,879
I use most often—hope the client sees this

20
00:00:41,879 --> 00:00:42,980
and sends funds right away.

21
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:44,249
The setup is quite simple.

22
00:00:44,249 --> 00:00:46,237
Just buy a Tencent Cloud Lighthouse server

23
00:00:46,237 --> 00:00:48,320
and set up the LLM APIKEY in App Management.

24
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:50,277
create a bot in the Feishu Open Platform,

25
00:00:50,277 --> 00:00:52,920
and enable the long-connection positioning mode there

26
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:54,688
go back and fill in Feishu bot's APP ID

27
00:00:54,688 --> 00:00:56,320
and secret key in the channel setup

28
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:57,938
then you can send messages via Feishu

29
00:00:57,938 --> 00:01:00,520
to trigger OpenClaw to run your commands in the background

30
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:03,930
Let's first look at this message—once sent via Feishu,

31
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:05,895
it was received by the OpenClaw service.

32
00:01:05,895 --> 00:01:08,099
The most important part here is the 'role' field.

33
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:09,384
the 'user' field holds

34
00:01:09,384 --> 00:01:11,066
what we say to the LLM, 'assistant'

35
00:01:11,066 --> 00:01:12,850
is the LLM itself and its responses,

36
00:01:12,850 --> 00:01:15,238
while 'toolResult' is the output from the Agent's '

37
00:01:15,238 --> 00:01:17,220
'brushing' tool—also directed at the LLM.

38
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:20,419
Now let's focus on how they communicated—first, the 'user' part,

39
00:01:20,419 --> 00:01:22,780
which is my question: 'How much RAM do you have?'

40
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:24,755
then the LLM provided its response,

41
00:01:24,755 --> 00:01:26,849
addressing the query from multiple angles,

42
00:01:26,849 --> 00:01:27,860
with the third point

43
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:30,340
clarifying that I meant system RAM on the server.

44
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:32,821
Then note the toolCall response below,

45
00:01:32,821 --> 00:01:34,112
which tells the Agent

46
00:01:34,112 --> 00:01:36,820
to assist with a tool call—do not reply yet.

47
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:39,640
the format is simple: method name and arguments

48
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:42,293
this 'exec' is a built-in tool in Open Cloud,

49
00:01:42,293 --> 00:01:43,955
used to run shell commands.

50
00:01:43,955 --> 00:01:47,339
The specific command is 'free -h', which checks system memory.

51
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:49,790
and that back-and-forth chat is now complete.

52
00:01:49,790 --> 00:01:54,291
here, we can see that this round used 14,032 input Tokens

53
00:01:54,291 --> 00:01:55,990
and 294 output Tokens.

54
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:58,319
and since it was first, no cache was used at all here

55
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,413
then we can switch to OpenRouter's backend

56
00:02:00,413 --> 00:02:02,656
and see that the TOKEN counts here match up.

57
00:02:02,656 --> 00:02:04,799
This round of chat cost us roughly 0.1 dollars.

58
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:06,351
but it's not done yet —

59
00:02:06,351 --> 00:02:08,184
after the Agent ran the command,

60
00:02:08,184 --> 00:02:10,253
it sent another message to the LLM,

61
00:02:10,253 --> 00:02:13,449
namely the toolResult, which is the output from free-h

62
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:15,314
then the LLM received this result

63
00:02:15,314 --> 00:02:17,180
and chose not to call any more tools,

64
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:18,643
delivering a final response —

65
00:02:18,643 --> 00:02:20,559
this was the message I got on my phone.

66
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:23,496
Below, we also list the Token cost for this conversation,

67
00:02:23,496 --> 00:02:25,920
and the two rounds on OpenRouter look like this.

68
00:02:26,090 --> 00:02:28,667
But here's a question—why did just asking 'how much RAM?'

69
00:02:28,667 --> 00:02:30,290
result in tens of thousand tokens?

70
00:02:30,610 --> 00:02:33,407
because OpenClaw has many system prompts that are injected

71
00:02:33,407 --> 00:02:35,240
right at the first turn of a new chat

72
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:38,037
we can find these files on the machine—like this Agent,

73
00:02:38,037 --> 00:02:40,680
and inside the .md file there are loads of commands

74
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:42,210
This is the translation.

75
00:02:42,210 --> 00:02:43,596
You can read it carefully

76
00:02:43,596 --> 00:02:44,943
and see — an Agent's power

77
00:02:44,943 --> 00:02:48,199
largely stems from stacking countless prompt instructions.

78
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:50,788
even the file-read and file-write actions here

79
00:02:50,788 --> 00:02:53,480
are injected directly using plain, natural language.

80
00:02:53,870 --> 00:02:56,066
Now let's try something slightly more complex —

81
00:02:56,066 --> 00:02:58,549
compile today's AI news into a PDF and send it to me.

82
00:02:58,550 --> 00:03:00,630
then let's see how it does this task in the backend system

83
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:02,428
This time I used Minimax 2.1,

84
00:03:02,428 --> 00:03:04,767
since it shows the thinking process —

85
00:03:04,767 --> 00:03:08,339
the initial prompt was still the one I posted on Feishu.

86
00:03:08,380 --> 00:03:11,277
then the assistant and tool calls worked together,

87
00:03:11,277 --> 00:03:12,473
looping with the LLM

88
00:03:12,473 --> 00:03:14,353
and repeatedly asking the Agent

89
00:03:14,353 --> 00:03:16,660
to use tools—over 70 exchanges in total

90
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:18,292
if we look at all the 'thinking' fields,

91
00:03:18,292 --> 00:03:20,500
we can tell roughly what happened throughout the process.

92
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:23,625
First, a blunt web_search was done, straight to Toutiao,

93
00:03:23,625 --> 00:03:26,230
and then it tried to convert the content to PDF.

94
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:28,104
then, during the PDF conversion,

95
00:03:28,104 --> 00:03:31,341
he realized he needed to turn this markdown file into PDF,

96
00:03:31,341 --> 00:03:34,300
and create a new one in his language as time went on.

97
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:35,900
then the styled files.

98
00:03:39,170 --> 00:03:41,436
I tried my hardest and finally messed it up,

99
00:03:41,436 --> 00:03:43,290
but Feishu sending caused new issues

100
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:45,818
No worries, let's just head to the service zone.

101
00:03:45,818 --> 00:03:47,310
The Markdown file looks decent;

102
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:49,599
then we'll download this PDF and check it out.

103
00:03:51,630 --> 00:03:53,011
Let's try triggering OpenClaw

104
00:03:53,011 --> 00:03:55,589
now; another unique feature is setting up scheduled tasks.

105
00:03:55,910 --> 00:03:57,744
for instance, monitor someone's Twitter —

106
00:03:57,744 --> 00:03:59,389
notify me the moment there's a post

107
00:03:59,590 --> 00:04:02,242
In the backend, we just added a cron config file —

108
00:04:02,242 --> 00:04:05,003
each time, it sends these prompt words to the Agent

109
00:04:05,003 --> 00:04:06,274
at set times and dates.

110
00:04:06,274 --> 00:04:07,269
No magic involved.

111
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:08,869
Let's check the status file

112
00:04:08,869 --> 00:04:11,440
it generated—it's used to compare two tweet versions

113
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:14,275
Just open one file—oh, the timestamp is way off;

114
00:04:14,275 --> 00:04:16,559
no need to check the rest of the work then.

115
00:04:16,870 --> 00:04:18,692
Let's check his startup logs again —

116
00:04:18,692 --> 00:04:20,721
still like a determined, stubborn intern,

117
00:04:20,721 --> 00:04:22,389
pushing hard to finish the task

118
00:04:22,590 --> 00:04:23,923
would mangle the whole system

119
00:04:23,923 --> 00:04:25,670
just to finish this task at all costs

120
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:27,081
again, the APIs failed,

121
00:04:27,081 --> 00:04:29,005
return values were off, and install

122
00:04:29,005 --> 00:04:31,100
commands kept erroring out and so on

123
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:33,983
I would have preferred he told me right at first, 'Hey,

124
00:04:33,983 --> 00:04:36,140
there might be a better way to do this'

125
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:38,960
like using an RSS dot APP kind of tool

126
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:41,131
but it's paid—so if it does cost money,

127
00:04:41,131 --> 00:04:42,324
let's discuss it first,

128
00:04:42,324 --> 00:04:44,485
rather than stay silent when issues arise

129
00:04:44,485 --> 00:04:45,540
and just push ahead

130
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:48,020
Do you dread working with folks like that in your job too?

131
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:50,559
How would you describe today's Agents?

132
00:04:50,559 --> 00:04:53,309
Let me give you a wild yet vivid example to explain.

133
00:04:53,390 --> 00:04:53,750
For instance

134
00:04:53,750 --> 00:04:55,908
your boss once asked you what day it is today,

135
00:04:55,908 --> 00:04:57,539
and then you treated it as a task

136
00:04:57,900 --> 00:04:59,067
I need to check the date,

137
00:04:59,067 --> 00:05:01,500
so I pull out my phone and look at the time on it.

138
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:04,116
then you realize your phone is out of power,

139
00:05:04,116 --> 00:05:04,861
so you think, 'My

140
00:05:04,861 --> 00:05:07,296
phone is dead—I need to find a Power Up charger,'

141
00:05:07,296 --> 00:05:08,389
but you can't find one

142
00:05:08,510 --> 00:05:11,253
you thought then, I need to buy a Power Up cable,

143
00:05:11,253 --> 00:05:12,830
so you headed to the store

144
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:15,098
then you noticed the supermarket was closed,

145
00:05:15,098 --> 00:05:16,369
and after thinking it through,

146
00:05:16,369 --> 00:05:18,080
you realized you could try another one

147
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,468
so you hailed a cab to the other store,

148
00:05:20,468 --> 00:05:22,200
and when you stepped out, you sighed

149
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:24,123
my phone was dead, so I couldn't pay,

150
00:05:24,123 --> 00:05:26,053
and I had no loose change left—so you figured

151
00:05:26,053 --> 00:05:27,640
you needed to get cash from the bank

152
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:29,211
then you went to the bank,

153
00:05:29,211 --> 00:05:31,520
and after you drew cash, you found it wasn't enough

154
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:32,774
What could you do?

155
00:05:32,774 --> 00:05:34,007
You couldn't leave the cab.

156
00:05:34,007 --> 00:05:35,879
At this point, if it were an early Agent,

157
00:05:35,879 --> 00:05:38,089
it might have gotten stuck in an endless loop.

158
00:05:38,330 --> 00:05:40,050
and the current Agent might be a bit more clever

159
00:05:40,050 --> 00:05:40,050
Like,

160
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:42,317
I'll pawn my phone to the driver first,

161
00:05:42,317 --> 00:05:43,938
then deal with the rest later.

162
00:05:43,938 --> 00:05:45,611
Then you managed to exit the car,

163
00:05:45,611 --> 00:05:46,567
went to that store,

164
00:05:46,567 --> 00:05:48,959
and bought the Power Up charger successfully.

165
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:50,560
Ah, at this point you find your phone gone

166
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:52,520
wasn't the phone pledged to the driver then?

167
00:05:52,850 --> 00:05:54,152
then you realize you need a phone,

168
00:05:54,152 --> 00:05:55,569
but you don't have enough in the bank.

169
00:05:55,690 --> 00:05:57,057
then at this point you think,

170
00:05:57,057 --> 00:05:58,962
well, I guess I have to take out a loan,

171
00:05:58,962 --> 00:05:59,940
so you do just that

172
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:02,530
in the end, you spent the whole day on this,

173
00:06:02,530 --> 00:06:04,351
then tossed your old phone away,

174
00:06:04,351 --> 00:06:06,545
racked up a bunch of loans at the bank,

175
00:06:06,545 --> 00:06:08,280
and bought yourself a new one

176
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:11,366
Finally replied to boss: Oh, today is Feb 6th.

177
00:06:11,366 --> 00:06:13,259
Then the boss asked you again:

178
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:15,087
I sent you a file yesterday;

179
00:06:15,087 --> 00:06:16,325
could you translate it?

180
00:06:16,325 --> 00:06:18,101
But the file is on your old phone,

181
00:06:18,101 --> 00:06:20,148
and you never migrated those messages.

182
00:06:20,590 --> 00:06:21,281
and right then

183
00:06:21,281 --> 00:06:23,950
you started thinking, 'I need to get my old phone back'

184
00:06:24,230 --> 00:06:26,070
so you called that driver on the phone

185
00:06:26,230 --> 00:06:27,830
I won't fabricate the rest now.

186
00:06:28,030 --> 00:06:29,390
but you must have noticed the flaw

187
00:06:29,390 --> 00:06:32,272
that is, though each step of his reasoning was sound —

188
00:06:32,272 --> 00:06:34,420
he'd spot a problem and then solve it

189
00:06:34,570 --> 00:06:36,631
if in real life someone actually did this,

190
00:06:36,631 --> 00:06:38,290
you'd likely think them a madman

191
00:06:38,570 --> 00:06:40,042
and this person would charge you

192
00:06:40,042 --> 00:06:42,250
based on how much effort they themselves put in

193
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:43,820
Would you still use such folks?

